 | 2.5D | Bore (`bore`) | `ops/bore.rs` | Yes | No | Shipped (2026-10-22, geometry selection P6.6). A helix mills each picked circle to its drawn size: the tool centre runs on `r = (d - D) / 2`, so the wall stands at `d / 2`. The op cuts a circle larger than the tool and at most two tool diameters (one helix then reaches the centre, so no core is left). Under the default rule it leaves out a circle of the tool size, a smaller circle and a circle of more than two tool diameters, with one note each that names the fix ("drill it with a Drill op", "use a smaller tool, or a Drill with a tool of the circle size", "use a Pocket on the circle or a larger tool"); a picked misfit circle refuses by item. A point states no size, so a Bore does not cut it. The helix descends in whole turns of at most `pitch` (default 1 mm, the dressup helix pitch), counter-clockwise for `climb`, then cuts one turn on the floor. A `finish_allowance` above zero cuts the helix at `r - a`, then one floor turn and a helix up the wall at `r`. The floor is the op floor (`floor`, as Pocket); `Floor::StockBottom` gives a through hole. A flat or bull-nose end mill only. The helix runs at the ramp feed, else the plunge rate; the feeds rows are those of the 2.5D clearing family (Pocket, Roughing), and the calculator and the envelope read one helix turn (`pitch`) as the depth of the cut (P29-5b): a helix of pitch `p` engages `p` of material at its leading edge; the feeds matrix gains 40 Bore cells (16 ship: end mill and bull nose; 24 refuse), 1000 in all. The dressups that add an entry, a lead or a link are stripped. With stock source "remaining stock" each hole starts at the highest remaining stock within the hole radius of its centre, and a hole with no stock above its floor is dropped. The simulation sweeps the moves and the export writes them as moves (not a drill cycle). Sentry: `a_bore_cuts_the_stated_size_p6_6` |
 | 2.5D | Chamfer | `chamfer.rs` | Yes | No | Shipped. Runs on the Layered runner (W3.5, 2026-10-15): one level, `top_z - (w + t) / tan(a)`, from the tool angle. With stock source "remaining stock" the chamfer is skipped when no material stands within `w + t` of its tip path above that level (an edge a prior op removed); it is never clipped, because a clipped ring would start its bevel in the middle of an edge (G-RESTLEVELS claim 11). R-C and F21 (operator ruling 2026-10-23, SAFETY): each outline cuts one level below ITS OWN top: a drawing area below the op Top Z (as before), a mesh face below its plane under an auto top, a STEP edge below its Z. On a STEP model the Chamfer bevels the convex top edges only (`ItemUse::Edges`): the STEP door states the form of each ring, chain, circle and up face from the two faces at each segment (`selection::EdgeForm`); the default takes the convex top edge chains and rims (not the face rings, a departure from ruling Q3 for Chamfer only); a pick of a concave edge, of a bottom edge, or of any model edge in a Bottom or side setup refuses `NotAConvexEdge` by name. A model edge takes its material side from the model, so the Side dial applies to drawing areas only; an open edge is cut in its point order with no chord. Each item refuses `BuriedFace` against its own Z (fresh stock: the lowest item; remaining stock: each area and a band of `w + 2 t` round each edge). The model gouge guard reads a Chamfer (`Bevel`): a cut deeper than the bevel depth `w / tan(a)` below the V-bit CL Z gouges (an edge that runs into a wall); it does not see a cut into another face that is shallower than the bevel depth. Break: a STEP Chamfer default changes from the face rings to the convex chains. Sentry: `a_step_chamfer_cuts_convex_edges_only_rc` (`--features step`). |
